1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,500
Welcome to Module Twenty-Nine.

2
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:04,100
This one is optional.

3
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:07,400
It's a bridge between this course and your robotics course.

4
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:10,100
You know the reciprocal transplant experiment.

5
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:14,087
Move each population into the other's site and measure fitness.

6
00:00:14,087 --> 00:00:19,262
If a population does well at home but poorly away, you've found local adaptation.

7
00:00:19,262 --> 00:00:22,150
Home performance didn't predict performance elsewhere.

8
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:24,950
Robot learning lives on exactly that question.

9
00:00:24,950 --> 00:00:31,637
Controllers are developed in simulation, where trials are cheap, safe and endlessly repeatable.

10
00:00:31,637 --> 00:00:37,987
Then they're transplanted to a real robot, where friction, weights, cameras and cables are all a little different.

11
00:00:37,987 --> 00:00:41,462
The drop in performance has a name: the sim-to-real gap.

12
00:00:41,462 --> 00:00:45,287
Coding agents are now working on both sides of that transplant.

13
00:00:45,287 --> 00:00:48,650
The best source in the pack is a paper called SimEX.

14
00:00:48,650 --> 00:00:51,937
It points out two ways that agents fail with robots.

15
00:00:51,937 --> 00:00:58,600
If an agent writes robot code in one go, it has to guess things it can't know, like exactly where the camera is.

16
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:05,737
If it learns by trial and error on the real robot, every attempt costs robot time and risks a collision.

17
00:01:05,737 --> 00:01:09,700
So SimEX uses simulation as the agent's laboratory.

18
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:12,637
First, the agent explores in simulation.

19
00:01:12,637 --> 00:01:22,175
It invents its own test tasks, finds a weakness, and improves a small toolbox of code, along with a notes file of what it has learned and where its limits are.

20
00:01:22,175 --> 00:01:28,362
Several independent runs explore in parallel and share ideas, a bit like an island model.

21
00:01:28,362 --> 00:01:33,200
Then comes a tiny real-world phase, about ten minutes of robot time per task.

22
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:39,137
Each real failure is used to correct the simulator and to test fixes before the next real attempt.

23
00:01:39,137 --> 00:01:46,837
With that, the paper reports twenty-six successes out of thirty real trials, where the best comparison method managed three.

24
00:01:46,837 --> 00:01:49,600
The authors' key insight is worth keeping.

25
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:57,462
The simulator only has to get the physics roughly right, because what transfers is knowledge and procedure, not one finely tuned policy.

26
00:01:57,462 --> 00:02:01,587
A second source asks which data actually teaches a robot.

27
00:02:01,587 --> 00:02:08,137
Its answer: only data where the robot's own policy is acting contains the robot's own mistakes.

28
00:02:08,137 --> 00:02:12,825
Human demonstrations, however good, can't show you how your policy fails.

29
00:02:12,825 --> 00:02:14,900
Here's where the metaphor breaks.

30
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:17,700
Transplanted organisms keep adapting.

31
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:20,475
A controller trained in simulation doesn't.

32
00:02:20,475 --> 00:02:29,775
It carries a fixed gap to a world it never saw, and it may even exploit errors in your simulator, the same way a policy exploits a buggy reward.

33
00:02:29,775 --> 00:02:33,212
In the lab, you'll run your own transplant on your laptop.

34
00:02:33,212 --> 00:02:41,825
Your coding agent builds a two-joint arm in a free physics simulator, tunes a controller at home, and tests it on a hundred new targets.

35
00:02:41,825 --> 00:02:47,212
Then it moves the controller into a perturbed world and gives it five trials to adapt.

36
00:02:47,212 --> 00:02:53,700
Start with Part A. Choose three physical changes for your away site, and predict which will hurt most.

